module desugar
  source: ["desugar.ly"]
  why: "Seven-pass AST desugaring layer that runs BEFORE the checker. Lowers high-level Lyric constructs — interface inheritance, interface fields, relations, destructors, default-impl specialization, default-impl extraction, and field-access shorthand — into the simpler core forms (plain classes, fields, methods, impl blocks, and getter/setter calls) that the checker and lowerer expect."

  doc "Architecture":
    """
    desugar rewrites the parsed AST in place through seven ordered passes.
    Each pass produces AST that later passes consume; running out of order
    causes silent failures, so the fixed sequence in desugar_all is the
    single source of truth:

    0. desugar_interface_extends — materializes single-parent interface
       inheritance. `interface Child<A,B> extends Parent<A,B> { ... }`
       gets the parent's abstract methods, fields, default-method
       bodies, and destructor blocks copied into the child with
       parent's type-params substituted by the child's extends_args.
       Child wins on member-name collision. Extends chains resolve
       transitively (parent's own extends materializes first). Runs
       BEFORE pass 1 so inherited fields get their getter/setter pair
       synthesized on the child.
    1. desugar_interface_fields — turns `field T.name: Type` declarations
       on interfaces into matching getter/setter method signatures.
       The synthesized FuncDecls are tagged with is_synthesized=true so
       downstream passes (notably the checker's Phase 1.7 satisfaction
       check) can distinguish mechanical accessors from user-authored
       methods. The user-visible contract member is the field; the
       accessors exist for vtable correctness in the C backend.
    2. desugar_field_access — rewrites `obj.field` shorthand inside
       interface bodies into getter/setter method calls for fields lifted
       by pass 1.
    3. desugar_relations — runs in two phases:
         A. Skeleton synthesis: every relation decl finds-or-creates an
            impl block matching its (hint, parent, child) and drops
            per-side labels onto ib_args[i].label and the owns/refs
            flag onto impl.kind. No mapping/field generation.
         B. Ownership-impl materialization: every impl block with
            non-null kind whose interface declares hint shape gets its
            per-side fields injected onto the concrete classes named
            by ib_args[i].type_expr (label-prefixed) and FieldBind
            mappings appended (deduped against any user-authored
            mappings on the impl). A user-authored
            `impl Hint<A:l, B:l> owns { }` over a user-defined hint
            interface gets identical treatment to a relation-
            synthesized impl. Cross-block GLOBAL class lookup, see
            below.
    4. desugar_destructors — for each impl block with non-null kind
       (whether synthesized from a relation in pass 3A or written by
       the user as `impl Hint<A:l, B:l> owns { }`), deep-copies the
       interface's destructor blocks whose kind matches impl.kind
       onto each concrete class named by ib_args[i].type_expr.
       Multiple impls touching the same class append wrapped block-
       stmts. Method calls inside the copy are renamed to their
       label-prefixed forms using ib_args[i].label.
    4.5. desugar_specialize_default_impls — for each plain impl block
       (impl.kind == null) of a user-authored multi-class interface,
       emits a fully-substituted concrete copy of every default-bodied
       interface method as a top-level FuncDecl on the concrete
       receiver class. The impl's per-side type-arg bindings (G→Net,
       N→Route, E→Via, W→f32) are baked in via rich substitution at
       desugar time, so the monomorphizer never has to recover N and
       E from receiver-only type inference (which it can't). Skips
       methods the impl overrides via an explicit ImplMapping. Carries
       impl-level type-params (ib.imtp) onto the specialized fn so
       partial impls (`impl<W> Iface<..., W>`) remain generic in W.
       Panics on collision: two impls producing the same Class.method
       specialization is a user error (use distinct labels or provide
       an explicit alias binding in one impl). Sets `fn.source_impl =
       ib` on every specialized FuncDecl as a back-pointer to the
       producing impl, so the checker can consult the impl's alias
       bindings (ibm) when resolving dot-operator references inside
       the specialized body. See cr/docs/multi-class-interface-
       redesign.md §9 Phase 4 Wave 1 4w1-a.
    5. desugar_default_impls — two-path extraction for interface methods
       that carry a body:
       A. Erased path (interface has no field declarations AND no
          explicit impl blocks): deep-copies each default method via
          deep_copy_func_decl into a concrete free function with
          name `{Iface}_{Member}_{method}`. Sets receiver_type=null,
          converts the self param to an explicit param typed
          QualifiedType(iface.name, member_name). No type params or
          where clause added. The original method is KEPT in the
          interface with its body intact — the checker's
          check_structural_chase skips methods with bodies (defaults
          that don't need satisfaction by concrete classes).
       B. Non-erased / has-impl path (interface has field declarations
          OR has explicit impl blocks): existing behavior — extracts
          the method in-place as a generic function, copies interface
          type params onto the function, adds a relational where
          clause so any conforming class picks it up. For interfaces
          with impl blocks, pass 4.5 specializes default methods
          onto concrete classes first.

    All five passes complete before the checker runs; the checker therefore
    only ever sees plain classes, fields, methods, and impl blocks.

    Two substitution strategies coexist. The simple variant
    (Dict<Sym, string>, substitute_type_params_in_*) replaces type names
    as strings and is used for field injection and the lighter rewrites.
    The rich variant (Dict<Sym, TypeExpr>, substitute_type_params_rich_in_*)
    preserves full type arguments through substitution and is required in
    desugar_destructors, where generic types like Dict<K, V> must keep
    their type args intact across the copy.

    Deep copy underpins passes 4 and 5. deep_copy_block / _stmt / _expr /
    _type_expr / _type_args reconstruct the AST without sharing any Data
    pointers. Sharing pointers causes cross-relation contamination once
    method renames mutate the copied body. deep_copy_type_args in
    particular must clone the TypeArgs slice on CallExpr / MethodCallExpr,
    because rich type-parameter substitution rewrites TypeArgs in place;
    without the clone, the last relation processed wins for all
    destructors. Lock statements are a known gap in deep_copy_stmt
    (checker name collision between Lock() and TypeKind.Lock), but they
    do not appear in destructor bodies in practice.
    """

  invariant "Pass Order":
    procedural
    """
    desugar_all MUST invoke the passes in this exact order:
      0. desugar_interface_extends
      1. desugar_interface_fields
      2. desugar_field_access
      3. desugar_relations
      4. desugar_destructors
      4.5. desugar_specialize_default_impls
      5. desugar_default_impls

    Each pass generates AST consumed by later passes. Running out of
    order produces silent failures (missing fields, unrenamed method
    calls). All seven passes MUST complete before the checker runs.

    Pass 4.5 MUST run BEFORE pass 5 because pass 5 moves bodied
    FuncDecls off the interface; pass 4.5 deep-copies from them and
    needs them still attached.
    """

  invariant "Cross-Block Class Lookup":
    procedural
    """
    desugar_relations MUST resolve target classes using a GLOBAL lookup
    across ALL blocks, not a per-block classIdx. Relations routinely
    reference a class declared in a different block (e.g.
    `Lexer:lc owns [Comment:lc]` where Comment lives in ast.ly but the
    relation lives in lexer.ly).

    The Go implementation uses a `classLoc` map keyed by
    (blockIdx, classIdx); the bootstrap must mirror that pattern by
    scanning every block when injecting fields and generating
    label-prefixed impl method renames.

    Bug symptom of a per-block lookup: missing label-prefixed methods
    (e.g. `lc_children` never generated for Comment), which then causes
    the monomorphizer downstream to emit wrong types.
    """

  invariant "Deep Copy in Destructors":
    procedural
    """
    desugar_destructors MUST deep-copy interface destructor bodies onto
    each concrete class. Sharing Stmt/Expr Data pointers across copies
    causes cross-relation contamination once destructor method calls are
    renamed to their label-prefixed forms (e.g. `children` →
    `fb_children`).

    deep_copy_expr and deep_copy_stmt MUST also clone the TypeArgs slice
    on CallExpr and MethodCallExpr, because rich type-parameter
    substitution mutates TypeArgs in place. Without the clone, the LAST
    impl processed wins for ALL destructors.

    The pass iterates impl blocks with non-null kind — NOT relations.
    This makes user-authored
    `impl Hint<A:l, B:l> owns { }` over a user-defined hint interface
    get identical destructor synthesis as a relation-synthesized
    ownership impl. Concrete classes come from ib_args[i].type_expr;
    labels for method renaming come from ib_args[i].label; the
    owns/refs gating compares db.kind against impl_block.kind. See
    cr/docs/multi-class-interface-redesign.md §3.9.
    """

  invariant "Type Parameter Substitution Strategy":
    procedural
    """
    Two substitution strategies coexist and MUST be used as follows:
      - Simple — substitute_type_params_in_* / substitute_type_expr_copy
        with Dict<Sym, string>. Replaces type names as strings. Used for
        field injection and basic rewrites where only the top-level name
        matters.
      - Rich — substitute_type_params_rich_in_* with Dict<Sym, TypeExpr>.
        Preserves full type arguments through substitution. REQUIRED in
        desugar_destructors so that generic types like Dict<K, V> keep
        their type args intact across the copy.

    Mixing them — using the simple variant in destructor copy — drops
    type args and produces ill-typed AST that the checker rejects with
    confusing 'expected Dict<K, V>, got Dict' errors.
    """

  invariant "AST Reconstruction Gaps":
    procedural
    """
    deep_copy_stmt cannot reconstruct Lock statements due to a name
    collision in the checker between Lock() and TypeKind.Lock.
    deep_copy_expr cannot reconstruct Match expressions due to a name
    collision between Match() and StmtKind.Match.

    These are known limitations. They do not affect destructors in
    practice — destructor bodies contain neither lock statements nor
    match expressions — but any future pass that deep-copies arbitrary
    bodies must either avoid Lock/Match or extend deep_copy_stmt /
    deep_copy_expr first.
    """

  invariant "Erased Interface Default Method Extraction":
    procedural
    """
    desugar_default_impls treats erased interfaces (method-only, no
    field declarations) WITHOUT explicit impl blocks differently from
    the general case:

    Erased path: deep-copies each default method via deep_copy_func_decl
    (with an empty type_map to avoid aliasing bugs) into a concrete free
    function. The copy is renamed to `{Iface}_{Member}_{method}`, its
    receiver_type is set to null, and its self param is converted to an
    explicit param typed QualifiedType(iface.name, member_name). No type
    params or where clause are added. The original method is KEPT in the
    interface with its body intact — stripping the body would break the
    checker's check_structural_chase, which skips methods with bodies
    (default methods that don't need to be satisfied by concrete
    classes).

    Has-impl path: when the erased interface HAS explicit impl blocks,
    pass 4.5 (desugar_specialize_default_impls) handles specialization
    onto concrete receiver classes. Pass 5 then uses the old generic
    path (copy interface type params, add relational where clause) so
    the generic version remains available for unspecialized callers.

    The deep copy in the erased path is REQUIRED. Without it, the
    original FuncDecl is mutated (receiver_type set to null, self param
    rewritten), which corrupts the interface's own method list and
    breaks checker satisfaction for other conforming classes.
    """

  invariant "Impl Block Merging":
    procedural
    """
    When desugar_relations generates an impl block whose interface name
    and type args match a user-written impl block already on the class,
    the generated method-name mappings MUST be merged into the existing
    impl block rather than emitted as a duplicate. This lets user code
    provide explicit mappings while relation-generated mappings fill in
    the rest. Two impl blocks for the same (interface, type args) pair
    on one class is a bug, not a feature.
    """

  invariant "Field Name Mangling":
    procedural
    """
    Fields injected into concrete classes by desugar_relations Phase B
    are mangled with a double-underscore prefix (e.g. `__label_field`)
    to prevent direct access and avoid collisions with user-defined
    members. The dotted-scope sugar (e.g. `team.roster.children`) is the
    intended user-visible path; raw `__label_field` names are internal.
    """

  invariant "Per-Type-Var Labels on Synthesized Impls":
    procedural
    """
    When desugar_relations synthesizes a new impl block from a relation,
    or merges into an existing one, it MUST drop labels into per-type-
    arg ImplTypeArg slots (parent label → ib_args[0].label, child label
    → ib_args[1].label) NOT into a single impl-block-wide label slot
    (which no longer exists). Existing labels on the impl-type-args take
    precedence over the relation's labels — user-authored impls win over
    relation-synthesized labels.

    Per-side labels are also part of an impl's IDENTITY for the find-
    or-create lookup in Phase A. Two relations sharing the same (hint,
    parent_class, child_class) but differing in their labels (e.g.
    `DoublyLinked Node:fwd refs [Edge:a]` vs
    `DoublyLinked Node:bwd refs [Edge:b]`) MUST produce two
    independent impl blocks. The matcher therefore merges with an
    existing impl only when each side's existing label is either
    unset (slot still open for a label drop) or exactly equal to the
    relation's label. Without this rule, the second relation's labels
    get silently dropped on the floor and only the first relation's
    label-prefixed fields are injected, producing "field 'bwd' not
    found" errors at use sites. See cr/docs/multi-class-interface-
    redesign.md §3.8.
    """

  invariant "Ownership Annotation on Synthesized Impls":
    procedural
    """
    desugar_relations runs in two phases. Phase A (per-relation) finds
    or creates the impl block matching (hint, parent, child) and drops
    rel.kind into impl.kind plus per-side labels onto ib_args[i].label.
    User-authored values win: if the existing impl already carries a
    non-null kind, keep it (two relations merging into one impl with
    conflicting kinds is a contradiction; a checker diagnostic catches
    this when the validator generalizes in a follow-on commit).

    Phase B (per-impl) iterates impl blocks with non-null kind whose
    interface declares hint shape (one or more `field T.name: Type`
    decls). For each iface field, the slot whose name matches the
    field's type_param selects an ImplTypeArg; its type_expr names the
    concrete class to inject into and its label optionally prefixes the
    injected field name. Phase B reads everything off the impl, so a
    user-authored `impl Hint<A:l, B:l> owns { }` over a user-defined
    hint interface gets identical treatment to a relation-synthesized
    impl. New FieldBind mappings are buffered into a local slice and
    appended after the per-field loop completes, so that mid-loop
    reallocation of the impl's mapping backing cannot invalidate the
    `existing_mappings` snapshot used for user-binding dedup. Field
    injection is unconditional (the class field is what user-authored
    FieldBind mappings POINT AT — deduping it on mapping presence
    would leave the mapping dangling); mapping injection dedupes
    per-mapping against user-authored bindings.

    See cr/docs/multi-class-interface-redesign.md §3.9.
    """

  invariant "Phase C Populates ClassDecl.css":
    procedural
    """
    desugar_relations Phase C runs after Phase B inside the same per-
    block loop. For every impl block with non-null kind, it walks
    ib_args and appends one SubScope record onto each LABELED side's
    bound ClassDecl.css. Side index (0 = parent, 1 = child) and the
    hint interface's name are captured so the checker can emit a
    precise collision diagnostic (Checker.validate_subscope_collisions
    + the inner-class branch in register_class) when a user-declared
    field or method on the bound class shares a name with the label.

    This is Tier 1 of the sub-scope refactor (cr/docs/sub-scope-
    refactor.md) — METADATA ONLY. The injected `__label_member` field
    and method names produced earlier in Phase B remain on
    ClassDecl.cf / .cm under their mangled names; the SubScope is a
    side table, not a member container. Future tiers (NOT scheduled)
    would migrate the injected members into the SubScope itself.
    """

  doc "Public API and Function Roles":
    """
    Entry point:
      desugar_all — orchestrates the seven-pass pipeline on a File AST.

    Pass functions (in execution order):
      desugar_interface_extends — Pass 0: materializes interface
        inheritance by copying parent members with type-param
        substitution. resolve_extends handles transitive chains.
        deep_copy_func_decl copies a FuncDecl with simple substitution.
      desugar_interface_fields — Pass 1: converts interface field decls
        into synthesized getter/setter method signatures.
      desugar_field_access — Pass 2: rewrites obj.field shorthand into
        getter/setter calls. Delegates to rewrite_field_access_block /
        _stmt / _expr for recursive traversal.
      desugar_relations — Pass 3: multi-phase relation materialization
        (Phases A/B/C as described in the Architecture doc).
      desugar_destructors — Pass 4: deep-copies destructor bodies onto
        concrete classes, renaming method calls to label-prefixed forms.
      desugar_specialize_default_impls — Pass 4.5: creates specialized
        copies of default-bodied methods on concrete receiver classes
        using rich substitution. Sets fn.source_impl back-pointer.
      desugar_default_impls — Pass 5: two-path extraction of default-
        bodied interface methods. Erased interfaces (no field decls)
        without impl blocks: deep-copies via deep_copy_func_decl into
        concrete free functions with QualifiedType first param; original
        kept with body intact for checker satisfaction. Non-erased or
        has-impl interfaces: extracts in-place as generic functions with
        interface type params and relational where clauses.

    Deep-copy utilities (used by passes 4, 4.5, and 5):
      deep_copy_func_decl — full deep copy of a FuncDecl including all
      child arrays (params, type params, where clauses) and body. Used
      by pass 0 (inheritance) and pass 5 (erased default extraction).
      deep_copy_block / _block_val / _stmt / _expr / _type_expr /
      _type_expr_kind / _type_args / _expr_list — reconstruct AST
      without sharing Data pointers. deep_copy_type_args is critical
      for preventing cross-relation contamination.

    Simple substitution (Dict<Sym, string>):
      substitute_sym / substitute_type_expr_copy /
      substitute_type_params_in_type_expr / _block / _stmt / _expr —
      string-based type-param replacement for field injection.

    Rich substitution (Dict<Sym, TypeExpr>):
      substitute_type_params_rich_in_block / _stmt / _expr /
      _type_expr — preserves full generic type arguments. Required
      for destructors and default-method specialization.

    Method renaming:
      rename_method_calls_in_block / _stmt / _expr — maps generic
      interface method names to label-prefixed concrete forms
      (e.g. children → fb_children).

    Helpers:
      type_expr_name — extracts top-level Sym from a TypeExpr.
      sym_eq — nullable-safe symbol equality.
      rewrite_field_type_impl — substitutes interface type params with
        impl block's concrete type args on a field's type expression.
    """

